    c: 'Trichuris trichiura and Enterobius vermicularis both inhabit the large intestine as their primary habitat: Trichuris in the caecum and adjacent bowel, and Enterobius in the caecum, appendix and adjacent colon, with gravid females migrating out to the perianal region to deposit eggs. This shared large-bowel habitat contrasts with Strongyloides stercoralis, which instead inhabits the small intestine, chiefly the duodenum and jejunum. Recognising that both large-intestine-dwelling nematodes are correct together, rather than either alone, is the point of this question, and it is precisely why Strongyloides is excluded as the fourth option.',
    d: 'Strongyloides stercoralis inhabits the small intestine, not the large intestine, so it is not among the nematodes this question is asking about.',
